title: What else is a chair for?
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

I think I'm personally sort of drawn to Scandinavian mid-century modern, you know, like a [[Hans Wegner]], a [[Finn Juhl]], those kinds of chairs that are often tied to sort of the craft of high-end woodworking and joinery. Sort of highest quality, beautiful paper cordage or simple fabric, or just the beautiful wood itself. 

I'm also really interested in the other aspects of chairs themselves, right? Like just the idea of different ways people like to rest, repose, lean, lay on things. Continuing to explore that aspect of what a chair is, what a chair is for.

There are funny moments in culture, history, ergonomics, etc. that are also fun and interesting to think about. Like Kramer in Seinfeld talking about wanting all levels everywhere in his apartment that can both be seating but also serve other kinds of functional purposes. I know some ergonomic studies say that a lecture hall is better when it doesn't have seats and chairs but is made up instead of discrete platforms that people can use and sit on in different ways or lay on or access with other kinds of mobility devices

Where do these thoughts and exercises and outputs take my interest in chairs, [[Chris Attenborough]]'s interest in chairs?our interest in sculpture and building and art in general and design being practical, and just making fun of our realities sometimes.

* Source for the lecture-hall ergonomic studies (tiered platforms instead of seats) -> came from "The Chair" from Galen Cranz, need to confirm info

